  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "In This Criminal Petition vs Unknown on 26 September, 2019 Author: U. Durga Prasad Rao Bench: U. Durga Prasad Rao Hon'Ble Sri Justice U. Durga Prasad Rao Criminal Petition No.5750 Of 2019 Order:",
    "facts": "1. In this Criminal Petition, filed under Section 482 of Cr.P.C., the petitioner/accused No.5 seeks to quash the proceedings against him in Crime No.305 of 2019 of Pattabhipuram Police Station, Guntur Urban, which was registered against him and others for the offences under Sections 188, 272, 273, 328 and 420 of IPC and Sections 58, 59 and 63 of Food Safety and Standards Act, 2006 (for short, 'the FSS Act'). 2. The allegations against the petitioner/accused No.5, briefly, are that on 12.09.2019 at 04:00 p.m., when the complainant along with her staff including the mediators rushed to Syamala Nagar Railway Gate, Guntur, found A-1 and three others, who are selling the banned tobacco products in their buddy shops, having purchased the same from the petitioner/accused No.5, and on interrogation as the accused voluntarily confessed to have been engaged in the business of prohibited tobacco products, seized the same, registered F.I.R., remanded them to judicial custody for the offences stated supra. 3. Learned counsel for petitioner while denying the FIR allegations would submit that even if the prosecution case is accepted to be true, tobacco products would not fall within the definition of Section 3(j) of FSS Act and therefore the police are not authorized to register the crime, investigate and file charge sheet and therefore registration of the FIR is contrary to the provisions of FSS Act. He UDPR,J relied upon the order of this Court in Criminal Petition Nos.3731 of 2018 and batch, dated 27.08.2018.",
    "arguments": "4. So far as the offence under Section 188 of IPC is concerned, as per Section 195(1)(a)(i) of Cr.P.C, no Court shall take cognizance of the said offence except on the complaint in writing of that Court or by such Officer of the Court as that Court may authorise in writing in this behalf, or of some other Court to which that Court is subordinate.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "4. So far as the offence under Section 188 of IPC is concerned, as per Section 195(1)(a)(i) of Cr.P.C, no Court shall take cognizance of the said offence except on the complaint in writing of that Court or by such Officer of the Court as that Court may authorise in writing in this behalf, or of some other Court to which that Court is subordinate."
  },
